Previous research on grammatical error correction has shown that the evaluation of even minor errors can significantly impact results. To address this, we propose a new approach that takes into account the prevalence of errors when evaluating systems. This method utilizes the Information-Theoretic Risk that measures the difference in error frequencies between system predictions and true corrections. To calculate this difference, we employ the Kullback-Leibler divergence to consider the distribution of errors. To make systems more robust to rare errors, we modify the risk measure, resulting in a system evaluation score that reflects error prevalence. Experimental results demonstrate that our approach leads to more accurate evaluations, with fewer false rankings of systems. Furthermore, our method shows potential for improving system robustness by incorporating the frequency of errors into training objectives. This suggests that incorporating the prevalence of errors can also enhance system performance on the task of grammatical error correction. To facilitate the use of our approach, we have developed an open-source implementation and will make it publicly available upon paper acceptance. To our knowledge, this is the first risk metric tailored for the specific needs of grammatical error correction. To our knowledge, this is the first risk metric tailored for the specific needs of grammatical error correction. Our research code and data are available at https://github.com/nlpcho/gec-itr. The corresponding bibtex entry is provided below. 
