Recent studies have shown that differential privacy can be impractical in large-scale machine learning due to significant efficiency losses. In contrast, private optimization methods have improved significantly in speed compared to differential privacy. Although private optimization techniques have been widely studied, there is a lack of comprehensive understanding regarding their performance across different problem settings. In this study, we conducted an extensive empirical analysis across 11 diverse settings to compare the performance of differential privacy and private optimization techniques. Our results indicate that private optimization techniques consistently outperform differential privacy, particularly in simple problem settings. Furthermore, our analysis reveals that private optimization techniques can be outperformed by differential privacy in highly sensitive data, such as text, and simple image datasets, but differential privacy's advantage diminishes in more complex datasets, such as image and speech recognition. The findings suggest that private optimization techniques are a more practical choice for private machine learning in many scenarios. The code used in this study is publicly available at https://github.com/kraser/private-optimization-benchmark. The study's code was run on a server with 8 NVIDIA 1080ti GPUs and 1TBytes of RAM. The results for each setting are available in the table below. The results for each setting are available in the supplementary materials. The results for each setting are available in the supplementary materials. The results for each setting are available in the supplementary materials. The results for each setting are available in the supplementary materials. The results for each setting are available in the supplementary materials. \end{document} 
